/* Wau-Bun — Part 2, the same scenes retold in a tauter, more
   immediate voice: the events, people, places and turns of the 1856 text,
   in present-day English, with nothing added. Original prose written for
   this app — nothing is quoted from any other book.
   Generated — do not hand-edit; the passages live in wau-bun/dark/<scene>.txt.
   Loaded only when a reader opens this mode. */
var WAUBUN_DARK_PART2 = {
  p2s1: ["The evening of the 7th of April, 1812. Mr. Kinzie had his violin out and his children were dancing to it in front of the fire. Tea was laid. They were only waiting on their mother, who had walked a quarter of a mile up the river to sit with a neighbor who was ill.", "Then the door came open and she was in the room, pale, barely able to get the words out. \"The Indians! the Indians!\"", "They all spoke at once. \"The Indians? What? Where?\"", "\"Up at Lee's Place, killing and scalping!\"", "It took her a moment to steady herself enough to tell it. She had been up at Burns's when a man and a boy were spotted on the far bank, running downstream as hard as they could go. They shouted across the water for the Burns family to save themselves — the Indians were at Lee's Place, and the two of them had only just got out of it. Then they kept on for the fort, which stood on their own side of the water.", "After that there was nothing in the house but alarm and dismay. The family were bundled into two old pirogues that lay moored near the door, and paddled over as fast as the paddles would take them, to shelter inside the fort.", "Everything the man and the boy had been able to tell was soon known. To follow any of it, though, you have to know the ground.", "Lee's Place — Hardscrabble, in later years — was a farm with the Chicago River running through it, some four miles above the mouth. The farm-house stood on the west bank of the south branch. Down on the north side of the main stream, close to where it meets Lake Michigan, was the Kinzie home-house and trading establishment, described already.", "The fort faced it across the water, on the southern bank: the river, with a few rods of green turf sloping to it on each side, was the whole of what lay between the two.", "It was not the fort that went up on the same ground in 1816. This one had two block-houses on its southern side, and on its northern a sally-port — a passage running underground from the parade to the river. The point of it was that people could get out that way in an emergency, or that the garrison could be kept in water through a siege."],
  p2s2: ["The officers at the fort just then were Captain Heald, who commanded; Lieutenant Helm, Mr. Kinzie's son-in-law; Ensign Ronan; and the surgeon, Dr. Van Voorhees. Helm and Ronan were both very young men.", "The command came to about seventy-five men. Very few of them were effective.", "Between the garrison and the Indians there had been steady, friendly dealing all along. Certain things might have been set against that. Every year the leading men of the Pottowattamie nation, like the leading men of most tribes, made the journey up to Fort Malden in Canada and came back loaded with presents; the British government had been buying their alliance that way for years. It was also well enough known that a good many Pottowattamies, and Winnebagoes with them, had fought beside the Ottawas and Shawnees at Tippecanoe the autumn before. But the head chiefs of every band in the neighborhood were on the friendliest possible terms with the Americans, and nobody looked for the peace to break.", "It was after the 15th of August that people started to remember. There had been a whole run of circumstances that would have opened the settlers' eyes, if the settlers had not been so comfortably sure of themselves. One will do for an example.", "In the spring before the fort was destroyed, two Indians of the Calumet band came in to pay a call on the commanding officer. Crossing the quarters, they passed Mrs. Heald and Mrs. Helm at a game of battledoor.", "One of them, Nau-non-gee, turned to the interpreter. \"The white chiefs' wives are amusing themselves very much, it won't be long before they are hoeing in our corn-fields!\"", "Nothing was made of it at the time. It was taken for an empty threat, or at worst a flash of resentment at how unlike the lives of their own women were to the lives of the \"white chiefs' wives.\" Some months later they remembered it, and remembered it bitterly."],
  p2s3: ["The farm at Lee's Place was held by a Mr. White, who had three people working for him there to look after it.", "On the afternoon our story begins, ten or twelve Indians came up to the house, dressed and painted. They came in and sat down where they pleased, without waiting to be asked, which was the custom.", "Something about the look of them, and the way they carried themselves, put one of the household on his guard. He was a Frenchman. \"I don't like the appearance of these Indians - they are none of our folks. I know by their dress and paint that they aren't Pottowattamies.\"", "Another of the household was a discharged soldier, and he spoke to the boy who was standing there. \"If that is the case, we had better get away from them if we can. Say nothing, but do as you see me do.\"", "The afternoon was well along by then. The soldier walked down toward the bank without hurrying; two canoes were tied up there. Some of the Indians asked him where he was going. He pointed at the cattle, out among the haystacks on the far bank, and made signs that the beasts had to be foddered and that the two of them would be back for their supper afterward.", "He took one canoe and the boy took the other. The stream is narrow there and they were over in no time. On the other side they pulled hay down for the cattle and made a show of gathering them in, working round by slow degrees until the haystacks stood between them and the house. Then they went into the woods, which began right at hand, and ran for the fort.", "They had gone about a quarter of a mile when a gun went off behind them, and then a second one. They took it that both had been fired at the men they had left in the house."],
  p2s4: ["They did not stop and they did not rest until the current carried them opposite Burns's, where, as has been told, they shouted across the water to warn the family of the danger they were in. Then they went on to the fort.", "Once their own safety was secured, it came to them that the Burns family stood at that moment in the most imminent peril. Somebody would have to go back up the river for them. The question was who would put his own life down on the table to do it. A gallant young officer, Ensign Ronan, volunteered, and took a party of five or six soldiers with him.", "They worked a scow up the river. The mother had been delivered of a child scarcely a day before, so they lifted her into the boat on her bed, the infant with her, and carried her and the rest of the family carefully down to the fort.", "That same afternoon a corporal and six men had been given leave to go up the river to fish.", "They had not come back when the survivors of Lee's Place reached the fort. Since they might well fall in with the Indians up there, the officer in command had a cannon fired as a warning to them.", "They were two miles above Lee's Place when the signal reached them. It was night by then. They took the hint at once, put out their torches, and let the boat drop back down toward the garrison as silently as they could manage. Nothing about the country had felt settled since the battle of Tippecanoe the previous November, and every man in it had learned to read the smallest noise the same way. Beware of the Indians.", "Coming down past Lee's Place, they decided to put in and warn the people there to be on their guard, since the signal from the fort meant danger of some kind. Around the house everything was as still as death. They groped their way forward. The corporal swung a leg over the small enclosure and came down with his hand on the body of a dead man. By touch he learned that the head had been scalped, and that the body had been mutilated besides. The murdered man's dog was standing over him, keeping watch on what was left of his master.", "That was the whole tale. The men retreated to their canoes and reached the fort unmolested about eleven o'clock at night. The next morning a party of citizens and soldiers volunteered to go up to Lee's Place and learn what had become of the others. They found Mr. White with two balls in him and eleven stab wounds in his breast. They found the Frenchman where he had fallen, the dog still beside him. Both bodies were carried down to the fort and buried close under its walls.", "It came out later, from traders out in the Indian country, that the men who did it were a party of Winnebagoes who had come into this neighborhood to take some white scalps. Their plan had been to work down the river from Lee's Place and kill every white man outside the walls of the fort. Then the cannon spoke. They did not know what it meant, and not knowing, they decided that this one exploit was enough, and went home to Rock River."],
  p2s5: ["The people who lived outside the walls — a few discharged soldiers, some families of half-breeds — moved into the Agency House and dug themselves in. It stood west of the fort, between the pickets and the river, about twenty rods off.", "It was an old-fashioned log building with a hall running straight through the center and one large room on either side. Piazzas ran the whole length of it, front and rear. These they planked up for greater security, cut port-holes through the planking, and posted sentinels there at night.", "The enemy was believed to be lurking still in the neighborhood, and if not, then likely to come back at any moment, having found the work easy the first time. So an order went out. No soldier and no citizen was to leave the vicinity of the garrison without a guard.", "One night a sergeant and a private walking patrol came around into the pasture adjoining the esplanade and found themselves among a party of Indians. The sergeant fired his piece and both men ran for the fort. They had not reached it when one of the Indians threw his tomahawk. It missed the sergeant and struck a wagon standing near. The sentinel in the block-house fired at once, and to effect, and the two men got safely in. In the morning they read what had happened in the grass: blood, a long way out into the prairie, and a place where the stalks were pressed flat and a body had been laid.", "Another time they came into the esplanade after horses. The stable had no horses in it. Rather than go away with nothing, they stabbed every sheep inside and turned them loose. The animals bolted toward the fort, and that is what raised the alarm. The garrison was aroused, parties were sent out after them, and the marauders got clean away, unmolested.", "After that the fort was left alone for many weeks."],
  p2s6: ["On the afternoon of the 7th of August, Winnemeg — Catfish — a Pottowattamie chief, came in to the post with despatches from General Hull. The despatches said three things. The United States and Great Britain were at war. General Hull had reached Detroit at the head of the Northwestern army. And the island of Mackinac had fallen into the hands of the British.", "The orders to Captain Heald were \"to evacuate the fort, if practicable, and, in that event, to distribute all the United States' property contained in the fort, and in the United States' factory or agency, among the Indians in the neighborhood.\"", "When he had handed over the despatches, Winnemeg asked to speak with Mr. Kinzie alone. Mr. Kinzie had by then moved his residence inside the fort. Winnemeg told him he knew what the papers said, and asked him to find out whether Captain Heald meant to evacuate the post. He advised strongly against it, and gave his reasons. The garrison was well supplied with ammunition, and had provisions enough to last six months. Far better, he thought, to stay behind the walls until a reinforcement could be sent to their assistance. But if Captain Heald did decide to leave, then he should leave immediately — that day, that hour. The Pottowattamies whose country they would have to cross did not yet know what Winnemeg had been sent to carry. A forced march might get the column through before the ones who wished them harm were ready to stop it.", "Captain Heald was told at once what Winnemeg had said, and how earnestly he had said it. He answered that he did intend to evacuate the post. But he had been ordered to distribute the United States' property, and he would not feel justified in leaving until he had gathered in the Indians of the neighborhood and made an equitable division among them.", "Winnemeg tried once more, and suggested another expedient. Then march out, he said, and leave every last thing standing where it was. While the Indians were busy dividing the spoils, the troops might get away unmolested. Mr. Kinzie seconded this strongly. It did not meet the approbation of the commanding officer."],
  p2s7: ["The order to evacuate the post was read out on parade the next morning. It is hard to understand why Captain Heald, with a thing like that in front of him, left out the usual form of calling a council of war with his officers. The only accounting for it is that there was no harmony between himself and one of his juniors — Ensign Ronan, a high-spirited and somewhat overbearing young man, though brave, and generous.", "By the middle of the day it was plain no council was going to be called. The officers went to Captain Heald themselves and asked what course he intended to take. When they heard it, they argued against it, on these grounds.", "First. There was almost no chance the command would be allowed to pass through the country to Fort Wayne unharmed. It was true that some of the chiefs had opposed an attack on the fort planned the previous autumn. But everyone knew why. They had acted out of private regard for one family, Mr. Kinzie's, and not out of any general good feeling toward Americans, and in any case it was too much to expect that a handful of men could hold back a whole tribe that was thirsting for blood.", "Second. The march would have to be slow, because it would move at the pace of the women and children, and there were a number of both with the detachment. Of the small force they had, some of the soldiers were superannuated and others were invalid. Since the course to be pursued had been left to the commanding officer's discretion, their advice, and it was unanimous, was to stay where they were and fortify as strongly as they could. Help from the other side of the peninsula might arrive before the British came down from Mackinac. And if it did not, it was far better to fall into British hands than to become the victims of the savages.", "Captain Heald answered that the War Department had issued a special order that no post was to be surrendered without battle having been given, and that his force was totally inadequate to an engagement with the Indians. He would unquestionably be censured for remaining, he said, when there appeared to be a prospect of marching through in safety. On the whole he thought it expedient to assemble the Indians, distribute the property among them, and then ask them for an escort to Fort Wayne, with the promise of a considerable reward when they arrived. He added that he had full confidence in the friendly professions of the Indians, from whom, as from the soldiers, the fall of Mackinac had been kept a profound secret.", "After that the officers held themselves apart and said little on the subject, though privately they thought Captain Heald's project little short of madness. Among the soldiers the dissatisfaction grew by the hour until it reached a high pitch of insubordination.", "Once, on the parade, Captain Heald was talking with Mr. Kinzie and said, \"I couldn't stay, even if I thought it best, for I have but a small store of provisions.\"", "A soldier standing near forgot every rule of etiquette in the heat of the moment. \"Why, captain,\" he said, \"you have cattle enough to last the troops six months.\"", "\"But,\" replied Captain Heald, \"I have no salt to preserve it with.\"", "\"Then jerk it,\" said the man, \"as the Indians do their venison.\""],
  p2s8: ["The Indians grew more unruly by the day. They came into the fort past the sentinels as though the sentinels were not there, and walked into the officers' quarters without ceremony. One of them picked up a rifle and fired it in the parlor of the commanding officer, for no reason but to show that he could. Some thought the shot was meant as a signal to the young men to begin. The old chiefs moved back and forth among the gathered groups in what looked like the liveliest agitation, and the squaws went to and fro in great excitement, plainly prepared for some fearful scene.", "Nothing came of it that day. The ill feeling was suppressed for the present. And Captain Heald — strange as it is to write — went on holding the conviction that he had created so amicable a disposition among the Indians as would carry his command safely to Fort Wayne.", "So the days went by until the 12th of August. What the people inside the fort felt in that time is better imagined than described. Every dawn seemed to bring them one day closer to the same appalling end, butchery by a savage foe, and at night they hardly dared let themselves sleep, in case they woke to the war-whoop and the tomahawk. Gloom and mistrust settled over everything. And because the officers were not of one mind, they were denied even the consolation of sympathizing with one another.", "The Indians came in from the neighboring villages, and a council was held with them on the afternoon of the 12th. Captain Heald attended it alone on the part of the military. He had asked his officers to come with him and they had declined. They had been told, secretly, that the young chiefs meant to fall on the officers and massacre them in the middle of the council, and they could not persuade Captain Heald that the information was true. So they waited only until he had walked out of the garrison with Mr. Kinzie beside him. Then they took command of the block-houses that overlooked the esplanade where the council was being held, opened the port-holes, and laid the cannon so that they covered the whole assembly, every man in it. That is probably the reason the whites who sat in that council are alive to be spoken of.", "In council, the commanding officer told the Indians that the next day he would distribute among them not only the goods lodged in the United States' factory, but the ammunition and the provisions too, of which the garrison had plenty. Then he asked the Pottowattamies for an escort to Fort Wayne, promising them a liberal reward on arrival, over and above the presents they were about to receive. With many professions of friendship and good will, they assented to everything he proposed, and promised everything he required."],
  p2s9: ["After the council broke up, Mr. Kinzie went to Captain Heald and talked with him a long time. Mr. Kinzie understood the Indian character, and he understood the particular temper of these Indians at this hour, and he hoped to open the captain's eyes to how matters actually stood.", "He reminded him that ever since the troubles on the Wabash there had been a settled plan of hostilities against the whites, and that because of it the policy of the Americans had been to withhold from the tribes anything that would help them carry that war to the defenceless people on the frontier.", "He reminded him, too, of his own conduct the previous autumn. He had set out for Detroit, and had got as far as De Charme's when word reached him of the battle of Tippecanoe. He turned around at once and came back to Chicago so that he could send orders to his traders to furnish no ammunition to the Indians. All they had on hand was hidden. Those traders who had not already left for their wintering-grounds went out carrying neither powder nor shot.", "Captain Heald was struck by the folly of arming the enemy — for that is what the old neighbors would have to be called now — against himself. He determined to destroy all the ammunition except what his own troops would need.", "On the 13th the goods were distributed as agreed: blankets, broadcloths, calicoes, paints. That evening the ammunition and the liquor were moved. Part went into the sally-port and down a well that had been dug there to supply the garrison with water in an emergency. The rest was carried out as quietly as they could manage through the northern gate, the barrel-heads knocked in, and the contents poured into the river.", "A large quantity of alcohol belonging to Mr. Kinzie went the same way. It had been stored in a warehouse near his house, across from the fort.", "The Indians suspected something was happening. They crept in serpent-like, as close to the work as they could get, but the watch was vigilant and no one was allowed near except the men engaged in it. Every musket not needed for the march was broken up and thrown down the well, and after them the bags of shot, the flints, the gunscrews — everything, in short, that had anything to do with a weapon of offense."],
  p2s10: ["Something lifted the general despondency a little on the 14th of August, when Captain Wells came in with fifteen friendly Miamis.", "Of this brave man, who stands so plainly in our frontier annals, it is enough to say here that he had lived among the Indians since he was a boy, and so knew their character and their habits perfectly.", "At Fort Wayne he had heard of the order to evacuate Chicago. He knew what the Pottowattamies had decided to do. He made a rapid march straight across the country to keep his relative, Captain Heald, and Captain Heald's troops, from walking into certain destruction.", "He came too late. All too late. When he reached the post he found the ammunition already destroyed and the provisions already given away to the Indians. There was no alternative left to anyone now. Every preparation was made for the troops to march out the following morning.", "That same afternoon a second council was held with the Indians. They expressed great indignation at the destruction of the ammunition and the liquor.", "For all the care that had been taken to keep it quiet, the sound of barrel-heads being knocked in had carried, and betrayed the whole night's work. And there had been so much liquor poured into the river that the water tasted of it in the morning. Strong grog, as somebody put it.", "Murmurs went through them, and threats after them. It was plain enough that the first moment the troops stood out in the open, they would be shown exactly how much that disappointment was resented."],
  p2s11: ["There were chiefs among them who shared the general hostile feeling of their tribe toward the Americans and yet kept a personal regard for the troops at this post and for the few white citizens of the place. Those men spent everything they had of influence trying to cool the revengeful feelings of the young men and turn them off their sanguinary designs. It did no good at all.", "On the evening after the council, Black Partridge, a conspicuous chief, came into the quarters of the commanding officer.", "\"Father,\" he said, \"I come to deliver up to you the medal I wear. It was given me by the Americans, and I have long worn it in token of our mutual friendship. But our young men are resolved to imbrue their hands in the blood of the whites. I can't restrain them, and I won't wear a token of peace while I am compelled to act as an enemy.\"", "If anyone in that devoted band had still needed proof that their melancholy anticipations were correct, they had it now. Even so, they went steadily on with the preparations that had to be made. And in the middle of all of it there were gallant hearts who worked at encouraging their desponding companions with hopes of escape they did not hold themselves.", "Of ammunition, twenty-five rounds had been reserved, and one box of cartridges in the baggage-wagons. Against any real danger it would have been an inadequate supply. But they had a fatiguing march ahead of them in the state they were in, and could not afford to burden themselves with more."],
  p2s12: ["The morning of the 15th came. Everything stood ready, and nine o'clock was the hour set for starting.", "Mr. Kinzie had offered to go with the troops on their march, and so his family had to be put in other hands. He gave them over to some friendly Indians, who undertook to bring them by boat to a point[34] on the St. Joseph's River, round the head of Lake Michigan, and there the troops would rejoin them if the troops were allowed to finish their march.", "Word reached him early that morning from To-pee-nee-bee, who was a chief among the band at St. Joseph's. The Pottowattamies who had contracted to escort the detachment meant mischief by it. The chief urged him to give up the idea of traveling overland with the soldiers, and pledged that the boat carrying him and his own people would be let through to St. Joseph's without harm.", "Mr. Kinzie would not take the offer. Most of those men held him and his family in real affection, and he believed that his being there among them might hold their fury in check.", "In the boat were Mrs. Kinzie; her four younger children; Josette, who nursed them; a clerk of Mr. Kinzie's; two servants; the boatmen; and the two Indians standing as their protectors. The boat pushed off. It had barely come down to the mouth of the river — half a mile below the fort, it will be remembered — when a second messenger from To-pee-nee-bee overtook them with instructions to hold exactly where they were and go no farther. There was only one thing that could mean.", "The wife and mother sat in the boat and could hardly breathe. She was a woman of uncommon energy, and of a strength of character that few could match. It did not help her. Her heart failed inside her while she gathered her arms around her helpless infants and watched her husband and her eldest child march away to certain destruction."],
  p2s13: ["The column came out of the fort and the band began to play. The Dead March. They came on in proper order, ranks correct, and not one face among them was anything but grave. Captain Wells was in front, leading his handful of Miamis. Before leaving the garrison he had blackened his face, a token of the fate coming for him. Their route ran along the lake shore. Where the beach met a low range of sand-hills standing between the water and the prairie, the Pottowattamie escort, about five hundred of them, did not keep to the sand. They held the prairie above, and the Americans and the Miamis went on alone below."],
  p2s14: ["They had gone perhaps a mile and a half when Captain Wells, who had ridden out in front with his Miamis, came back down the line at a gallop.", "\"They are about to attack us,\" he shouted, \"form instantly, and charge upon them.\"", "He had barely got the words out. The volley came down on them from among the sand-hills. The troops were pushed into line where they stood and sent up the bank at a charge, and one of them — a veteran of seventy winters — went down on the way up. What happened after that is better told by somebody who stood in the middle of it: Mrs. Helm, wife of Captain Helm, who was a lieutenant then, and step-daughter of Mr. Kinzie.", "\"After we left the bank, everyone was firing. The Miamis were gone at the first shot. Their chief turned his horse toward the Pottowattamies and spoke to them:\"", "\"'You have deceived the Americans and us. You have done a bad action, and (brandishing his tomahawk) I will be the first to head a party of Americans to return and punish your treachery.' Then he was gone after his own people, who were already streaking away over the prairies.\"", "\"The troops fought gallantly. There was only a handful of them, and they seemed to have decided to make the price of their lives as high as they could. Our horses would not stand; they pranced and bounded and could scarcely be held while the balls went by them. I pulled off a little way and looked at my husband and my father, both of them still untouched. I understood that my own hour had come. I tried to put every person I loved out of my mind, and to make myself ready for what was coming.\"", "\"While I was doing that, the surgeon, Dr. Van Voorhees, came up to me. He was badly hurt. His horse had been shot out from under him and he had taken a ball in the leg, and every muscle of his face was moving with the agony of terror. 'Do you think they will take our lives? I am badly wounded, but I think not mortally. Maybe we might purchase our lives by promising them a large reward. Do you think there is any chance?'\"", "\"'Dr. Van Voorhees,' I said, 'don't let us waste the few moments that yet stay to us in such vain hopes. Our fate is inevitable. In a few moments we must appear before the bar of God. Let us make what preparation is yet in our power.'\"", "\"'Oh, I can't die!' he exclaimed, 'I am not fit to die - if I had but a short time to prepare - death is awful!'\"", "\"Ensign Ronan was close by us, hurt past saving, almost on the ground, and still fighting on one knee with everything left in him.[35] I pointed at him. 'Look at that man!' I said. 'At least he dies like a soldier.'\"", "\"'Yes,' he answered, and the word came out of him as a convulsive gasp, 'but he has no terrors of the future - he is an unbeliever!'\"", "\"Then a young Indian brought a tomahawk down at me. I threw myself sideways, so that the blade meant for my skull landed on my shoulder instead. I got both arms around his neck. His scalping-knife hung in a scabbard over his breast, and I was working with everything I had to get hold of it when an older man took me and dragged me out of his grasp.\""],
  p2s15: ["\"He carried me toward the lake, struggling and resisting the whole way. Fast as I was being hurried along, I saw what we passed. The surgeon lay dead on the ground, on the exact spot where my eyes had last found him living, and some tomahawk had put him there.", "\"Then I was in the lake. A hand held me fast there, and I struggled against it, and could not move it. What I understood after a moment was that the hand was not trying to drown me. It had me fixed in a particular way, and the way it had me kept my head clear of the water. That steadied me enough to look at the man who held me, and once I looked I knew him, paint and all: The Black Partridge.", "\"The firing thinned out, and then he lifted me from the water and walked me up the sand-banks. It was August, and the morning was already burning. Wet through as I was, that climb across loose sand was more painful and more exhausting than I can put into words. My shoes had packed nearly solid with sand, and I bent down and pulled them off to empty them, and a squaw came and took them out of my hands and carried them away, and I went on barefoot.", "\"On the prairie my father met me and told me that my husband was alive and only slightly hurt. They brought me back gently toward the Chicago River. The Pottowattamie camp lay along its southern bank. Somebody put me on a horse with no saddle, but the motion of it was more than I could bear, and I threw myself off. So I went on foot instead, held up on one side by my kind conductor, Black Partridge, and on the other by an Indian named Pee-so-tum, who carried something swinging from his hand. It was a scalp. I knew whose it was by the black ribbon tied around the queue: Captain Wells. Fainting, I dragged myself as far as one of the wigwams.", "\"There was a woman standing near it, the wife of Wau-bee-nee-mah, a chief who had come from the Illinois River. She saw the state I was in. She took up a kettle and went to the stream that ran close by and dipped water, and dropped maple-sugar into it, and stirred it around with her hand, and put it into mine to drink. With everything that was happening around us, that small kindness went straight through me. But I could not hold on to it. There were other things to see.", "\"Whoever had stayed behind when the troops marched out had turned the fort over to plunder. The cattle, loose and running, had been shot down where they ran, and they lay dead and dying all about. That killing had started just as we were marching out. I remembered then what Ensign Ronan had said while the firing was going on. He turned to me. 'Such,' he said, 'is to be our fate - to be shot down like brutes!'", "\"The commanding officer heard him. 'Well, sir,' he said, 'are you afraid?'", "\"'No,' replied the high-spirited young man, 'I can march up to the enemy where you dare not show your face.' What he did afterward proved it was no boast.", "\"The shooting slackened away to nothing, and the stragglers of the winning side came drifting into camp, and from them I had confirmed what my father had told me in those few hurried words on the lake shore. The whites had surrendered. About two-thirds of their number were gone. The terms, made through the interpreter, Peresh Leclerc, were that the survivors' lives would be spared, and the lives of the women and children who remained, and that they would be delivered at one or another of the British posts unless traders in the Indian country ransomed them first. The wounded, it seems, were held to fall outside that agreement. When they were brought into camp, what followed was horrible.", "\"There was an old squaw there who had lost friends, or who had simply been worked past herself by the slaughter all around her, and she was possessed by a ferocity I can only call demoniac. She took up a stable-fork and set on one of them where he lay groaning and twisting with his wounds, the sun beating down on him and making them worse. Then Wau-bee-nee-mah did a thing I would not have expected from anyone in such a place. He stretched a mat between two poles and stood it between me and what was happening, so that I was spared most of the sight of it. I could not shut out the sound of the man. In the night that followed, five more wounded prisoners were tomahawked.\""],
  p2s16: ["It appears that when the first attack came, the Americans went straight at the men who had hidden themselves in the kind of ravine that runs between the sand-banks and the open prairie. Those men drew together into one body, and the fighting that followed was hard. By the end of it there were twenty-eight whites left alive. Those twenty-eight forced a way through and got up onto rising ground a short distance from the Oak Woods. To go on fighting was plainly useless. Lieutenant Helm sent out Peresh Leclerc, a half-breed boy in the service of Mr. Kinzie who had come along with the detachment and had fought manfully on their side, to ask for terms. The terms agreed were that every survivor should keep his life, and that they might be ransomed as soon as it could be arranged.", "While that was being settled, something else had already been done. Back near the sand-hills, in the middle of the engagement, one young savage had climbed up into the baggage-wagon where the children of the white families were sitting. There were twelve of them. He killed all twelve with his tomahawk. Captain Wells was fighting close enough to see it happen, and he called out, - \"Is that their game, butchering the women and children? Then I will kill, too!\"", "He pulled his horse's head round as he said it and rode for the Indian camp beside the fort, where their own squaws and children had been left.", "Several Indians came after him. He went down flat along his horse's neck and rode that way, loading and firing from that position, and twisting round now and then to fire at the men behind him. In the end their balls found him. The horse was killed and he himself was badly wounded. Winnemeg and Wau-ban-see reached him then and tried to keep him alive; they got him clear of the dead animal and were helping him along between them when another Indian, Pee-so-tum, came up behind him and stabbed him in the back, and that was the blow that killed him."],
  p2s17: ["One of the soldiers' wives took a resolution that deserves to be recorded. Her name was Mrs. Corbin. From the beginning she had said she would never fall into the hands of the savages, believing that their prisoners were always put to tortures worse than dying.", "So when a party came for her, to take her prisoner, she fought them with desperation and would not surrender, though they made signs to her that she would be safe and well treated. She let herself be cut to pieces rather than be taken. That is literally what happened.", "There was a Sergeant Holt who took a ball in the neck early in the engagement. Knowing he was badly wounded, he handed his sword up to his wife, who was on horseback near him, and told her to defend herself. Then he made for the lake to get out of the way of the balls. Mrs. Holt rode a very fine horse. The Indians wanted the horse, so they attacked her, meaning to bring her down off it.", "They fought her with the butt-ends of their guns only, because they did not want to kill her. She hacked and hewed at the pieces as they were thrust at her, this side, that side, this side again. In the end she broke loose from them and drove out into the prairie. They came after her, shouting and laughing, calling out now and then, \"The brave woman! don't hurt her!\"", "They caught her again eventually, and while she was busy with two or three of them in front, one got behind her and took her by the neck and dragged her off the horse — and she was a large and powerful woman. Their guns were hacked and ruined and several of them were cut badly, and still they seemed to regard her with nothing but admiration. They took her to a trader on the Illinois River, who returned her to her friends. She had received every kindness during her captivity."],
  p2s18: ["The members of Mr. Kinzie's family who had stayed in the boat near the mouth of the river were carefully guarded by Kee-po-tah and another Indian. They saw the smoke first. Then the blaze. Then the report of that first tremendous discharge reached them. After that it was all confusion. They knew nothing at all until they saw an Indian coming toward them from the battle-ground, leading a horse with a lady on it who appeared to be wounded.", "\"That is Mrs. Heald,\" cried Mrs. Kinzie. \"That Indian will kill her. Run, Chandonnai\" — to one of Mr. Kinzie's clerks — \"take the mule that is tied there, and offer it to him to release her.\"", "By this time her captor was working the bonnet off her head so that he could scalp her. Chandonnai ran up and offered him the mule as a ransom, and ten bottles of whiskey besides, as soon as they reached his village. The whiskey was a strong temptation.", "\"But,\" said the Indian, \"she is badly wounded — she will die. Will you give me the whiskey in any case?\"", "Chandonnai promised he would, and the bargain was made. The savage put the lady's bonnet on his own head. Some squaws tried to take her shoes and stockings and did not succeed. Then she was brought aboard the boat, where she lay moaning with the pain of the many bullet-wounds she had taken in both arms.", "The horse Mrs. Heald had been riding was a fine, spirited animal. The Indians wanted it undamaged, so they had aimed their shots to disable the rider without touching the steed.", "She had not been lying in the boat long when they saw a young Indian of savage aspect coming toward them. A buffalo robe was pulled hastily over her, and she was told to make no sound of complaint at all, if she valued her life.", "The heroic woman stayed perfectly silent while he came on. He had a pistol in his hand. He rested it on the side of the boat and looked in, prying, with a fearful scowl on his face. Black Jim, one of the servants, was standing in the bow. He took up an axe that lay near him and made a sign that if the man fired he would split his skull, and told him the boat held nobody but the family of Shaw-nee-aw-kee. The Indian withdrew. It came out afterward that the person he was looking for was Mr. Burnett, a trader from St. Joseph's, with whom he had some account to settle.", "When the boat was at last allowed to go back to Mr. Kinzie's mansion, and Mrs. Heald was carried up to the house, her wounds had to be dressed.", "Mr. K. asked an old chief who was standing by — who, like most of his tribe, had some skill in surgery — to take a ball out of the sufferer's arm.", "\"No, father,\" he answered. \"I can't do it — it makes me sick here.\" And he put his hand on his heart.", "So Mr. Kinzie did it himself, with his penknife."],
  p2s19: ["At their own mansion Mr. Kinzie's family were closely guarded by their Indian friends, whose intention was to carry them to Detroit for safety. The rest of the prisoners stayed at the wigwams of the men who had taken them.", "The next morning, the work of plunder being finished, the Indians set fire to the fort. The finery seemed to have been divided very equitably, and shawls and ribbons and feathers went fluttering about in every direction. One young fellow had got himself up in a muslin gown and one of the ladies' bonnets, and under any other circumstances the sight of him would have been funny.", "Black Partridge, Wau-ban-see and Kee-po-tah, with two other Indians, posted themselves in the porch of the building as sentinels, to keep the family from whatever the young men might be worked up to do. For a short while after the fort burned, everything stayed quiet.", "Then a party of Indians from the Wabash arrived. Of all the Pottowattamie bands these were decidedly the most hostile and the most implacable.", "Living farther off, they had shared less than the others in the kindness of Mr. Kinzie and his family, and their regard for that family was correspondingly thinner.", "Runners had been sent out to the villages to tell them the post was to be evacuated, and to tell them what the assembled Indians meant to do to the troops.", "They had come at speed, thirsting to be part of it, and at the river Aux Plaines they met a party of their friends coming the other way with their chief Nee-scot-nee-meg, badly wounded, and learned that the battle was over, the spoils divided, and every scalp already taken. Their mortification was great.", "Reaching Chicago, they blackened their faces and started for Mr. Kinzie's house.", "Black Partridge had been watching them come from his station on the piazza, and what frightened him most was Mrs. Helm, Mr. Kinzie's step-daughter, who had come to the post only recently and was not personally known to the more remote Indians. On his advice she was dressed as an ordinary Frenchwoman of the country — a short gown and petticoat, a blue cotton handkerchief wrapped around her head. In this disguise Black Partridge himself walked her over to the house of Ouilmette, a Frenchman with a half-breed wife who belonged to Mr. Kinzie's establishment and lived close by.", "As it happened, that was the first house the Indians came to, hunting prisoners. As they came up, the people inside were afraid her fair complexion and general look would give her away as an American. So they lifted a large feather bed and put her under the edge of it, on the bedstead, with her face to the wall. Then Mrs. Bisson, a half-breed, the sister of Ouilmette's wife, sat down on the front of the bed with her sewing.", "It was a hot day in August. Under the weight of the bed she could barely draw a breath, and the feverish excitement of fear on top of it grew so intolerable that Mrs. Helm at last begged them to let her out and give her up to the Indians.", "\"I can but die,\" she said. \"Let them put an end to my misery at once.\"", "Mrs. Bisson answered, \"Your death would be the destruction of us all, for Black Partridge has resolved that if one drop of the blood of your family is spilled, he will take the lives of all concerned in it, even his nearest friends, and if once the work of murder commences, there will be no end of it, so long as there remains one white person or half-breed in the country.\"", "That expostulation gave Mrs. Helm fresh resolution.", "The Indians came in. From her hiding-place she could see them at moments, gliding about the room, stealthily going over every part of it without appearing to search anything at all. At last, apparently satisfied that no one was concealed there, they left the house.", "The whole time, Mrs. Bisson kept her seat on the side of the bed, calmly sorting and arranging the patch-work of the quilt she was making, with the appearance of the utmost tranquillity — not knowing, from one moment to the next, whether she was about to receive a tomahawk in her brain. Her self-command unquestionably saved the lives of everyone in that room."],
  p2s20: ["From Ouilmette's house the party went on to Mr. Kinzie's. They came into the parlor where the family sat with their faithful protectors, and sat down on the floor without a word. Black Partridge read their looks — moody, bent on revenge — and knew what was in their minds. He did not argue. He did not dare to. He only leaned toward Wau-ban-see and said it low: \"We have tried to save our friends, but it is in vain — nothing will save them now.\"", "Then, across the river, a whoop. A friendly one. Black Partridge sprang out to meet their leader, and was down at the water as the hastily manned canoes touched the bank by the house.", "\"Who are you?\" he demanded.", "\"A man. Who are you?\"", "\"A man like yourself. But tell me who you are.\" He was asking the only thing that mattered: tell me your disposition, which side you are for.", "\"I am a Sau-ga-nash!\"", "\"Then make all speed to the house — your friend is in danger, and you alone can save him.\"", "Billy Caldwell — for it was he — walked into that parlor at an easy pace, nothing hurried in him, nothing in his face. He unbuckled his accoutrements without haste and set them with his rifle behind the door. Then he turned and saluted the hostile visitors.", "\"How now, my friends! A good-day to you. I was told there were enemies here, but I am glad to find only friends. Why have you blackened your faces? Is it that you are mourning for the friends you have lost in battle?\" He knew what blackened faces meant. He asked anyway.", "\"Or is it that you are fasting? If so, ask our friend, here, and he will give you to eat. He is the Indian's friend, and never yet refused them what they had need of.\"", "It caught them flat. Taken by surprise in that fashion, they were ashamed to own aloud what they had come to do. So they said modestly that they had come to beg of their friends a little white cotton, to wrap their dead in before burying them. It was given them, with some other presents. They gathered it up and left the premises peaceably."],
  p2s21: ["There was a non-commissioned officer in Mr. Kinzie's party who had got there by the strangest road of any of them. When the troops were forming to leave the fort, somebody found that the surgeon's baggage-horses had wandered off. They could not simply be left. Their packs carried part of the surgeon's apparatus and the medicines for the march, so they had to be recovered. The quartermaster-sergeant, Griffith, was sent back to collect them and bring them on.", "He had been a long time on the sick report. That was why he had the baggage instead of a place in the ranks. He could not find the horses. He was hurrying to catch up with his party, uneasy at signs of disorder among the Indians and at other signs that looked like hostility, when To-pee-nee-bee met him on the road and took him prisoner.", "The chief stripped him of his arms and accoutrements, put him in a canoe, paddled him across the river, and told him to get into the woods and hide himself. He did. The next day, in the afternoon, watching from his hiding-place and seeing that everything had gone quiet, he crept into Ouilmette's garden and got down behind the currant-bushes, and stayed there.", "Eventually he decided the house was safer than the garden. He climbed up through a small back window into the room where the family were — and his timing was very nearly the end of him, because it was the same hour the Wabash Indians were leaving Ouilmette's for Mr. Kinzie's. So they took the uniform off him. They dressed him in a suit of deer-skin with a belt, moccasins and a pipe, like a French engagé. His dark complexion and heavy black whiskers did the rest. Everyone in the house was ordered to speak to him only in French, and though he did not know one word of the language, he contrived to pass for a Weem-tee-gosh, and went on in that character with Mr. Kinzie and his family, under his enemies' eyes the whole way, until they were safe."],
  p2s22: ["Three days after the battle they loaded Mr. Kinzie's family and the clerks of the establishment into a boat and sent them off in the charge of François, a half-breed interpreter, down to St. Joseph's. There they remained until the November following, kept safe by To-pee-nee-bee's band. When they finally moved again it was to Detroit, with Chandonnai and their trusty Indian friend Kee-po-tah riding escort, and at the end of that road they were handed over as prisoners of war to Colonel McKee, the British Indian Agent.", "Mr. Kinzie did not go with them out of St. Joseph's. His Indian friends would not hear of it; he was to stay, and to try what could be salvaged of the property that had been scattered. When he went out with them on those errands he went dressed and painted as one of the tribe, because there were men still abroad with a thirst for blood in them, and a white man they recognized could be taken, or worse. In the end his fear for his family outweighed all of it and he followed them to Detroit, and there, in January, General Proctor received him and gave him his parole.", "Captain and Mrs. Heald went over the lake to St. Joseph the day after the battle. He had come out of the fight with two wounds in him. She had seven.", "Lieutenant Helm was wounded as well. Friendly Indians carried him to their village on the Au Sable, and from there he was taken to Peoria, where Mr. Thomas Forsyth, half-brother to Mr. Kinzie, stepped in and got him set free. Mrs. Helm had gone with her parents to St. Joseph. For several months they lived in the household of Alexander Robinson, and there was no kindness or hospitality that family did not show them.", "Her husband caught up with her after they reached Detroit, and the two of them were promptly arrested on the order of the British commander and sent off on horseback, in the depth of winter, across Canada to Fort George on the Niagara frontier. Nobody at that post had been appointed to receive them. After all those days of riding, in weather as cold and raw as that country can produce, Mrs. Helm — a delicate girl of seventeen — was left sitting her saddle outside the gate for more than an hour before anyone thought to offer her fire, or food, or so much as a roof over her head. Colonel Sheaffe had been away when it happened. When he was told of that piece of brutality he made no secret of his anger. He went to Mrs. Helm at once, apologized to her with the utmost courtesy, and from then until an exchange of prisoners set them at liberty he treated her and Lieutenant Helm with every consideration. They found their own way at last to their friends in Steuben County, N.Y.", "The man who had taken Captain Heald prisoner was an Indian from the Kankakee who was genuinely fond of him. Seeing how badly Mrs. Heald was wounded and how little strength she had left, he let her husband go so that he could travel with her to St. Joseph, and they were carried there by Chandonnai and his party, as has been told. Then he went home to his village on the Kankakee — and found that his own people were furious with him for what he had done. The anger of the band ran so high that he made up his mind to go to St. Joseph himself and take his prisoner back.", "Word of that reached To-pee-nee-bee and Kee-po-tah, who had charge of the prisoners, and they sat down in private council with Chandonnai, with Mr. Kinzie, and with the leading men of the village. What they decided was to put Captain and Mrs. Heald out of reach altogether: to send them to the island of Mackinac and hand them over to the British.", "So the two of them were placed in a bark canoe and paddled three hundred miles up the Michigan shore by Robinson and his wife, and at the end of it were surrendered as prisoners of war to the officer commanding at Mackinac.", "Something should be said here about Captain Heald's habit of putting things off. He had by then certain word that his Indian captor was coming from the Kankakee to St. Joseph to retake him — and even so he would have stayed one more day where he was, in order to make the trip to Mackinac a more comfortable one.", "As for the soldiers, and the wives and the children who had lived through it, they were parceled out among the villages of the Pottowattamies on the Illinois, on the Wabash, on Rock River, and at Milwaukie, and there they stayed until the spring, when most of them were brought in to Detroit and ransomed."],
  p2s23: ["Mrs. Burns and her infant became the prisoners of a chief who carried them to his village and treated them with great kindness. His wife did not. She saw the favor shown to \"the white woman\" and her child, and she hated them for it, and she made it plain every day. Once she struck the baby with a tomahawk, and came within very little of putting an end to it altogether. After that demonstration they were not left long in the old woman's power; at the first opportunity they were carried away to a place of safety.", "The Lees had lived in a house on the lake shore, not far from the fort. Mr. Lee owned Lee's Place and farmed it. It was his son who ran down to the fort with the discharged soldier on the afternoon of the 7th of April, shouting \"Indians.\" On the 15th of August the father, the son, and every other member of that family were killed, except Mrs. Lee and her young infant. Black Partridge claimed those two and took them to his village on the Au Sable. There had been another child, a girl of about twelve, whom he had been particularly fond of. She had been put on horseback for the march, and because she was not used to riding they had tied her fast to the saddle, so that she could not slip off or be thrown.", "She was inside the range of the balls when the firing started, and she was badly hit. The horse bolted. The fall half threw her, but the bands held, and she hung dangling from them while the animal ran wildly about. That was how Black Partridge found her. He caught the horse and got her loose from the saddle. She was hurt past any recovering, and she was in great agony, and he finished it there with his tomahawk. He said afterward that it was the hardest thing he ever tried to do. He said he did it because he could not stand to watch her suffer.", "He took the mother and the baby home to his village, and he came to care for her a great deal — enough that he wanted to marry her. She naturally objected. He did not press it. He treated her with the greatest respect and consideration instead, and he was in no hurry at all to release her, because he still hoped she would change her mind. That winter the child fell ill. Nothing they had for it did any good. So Black Partridge proposed to carry the little one down to Chicago, where a French trader was now living in Mr. Kinzie's house, and get some medical help from him. He wrapped his charge up with the greatest care and set out.", "He reached M. Du Pin's, went into the room where the trader was, and laid his bundle down on the floor, carefully.", "\"What have you there?\" asked M. Du Pin.", "\"A young raccoon, which I have brought you as a present,\" he said, and opened the pack, and there was the sick infant.", "The trader prescribed for the child's complaint. Then, as Black Partridge was preparing to start home, the chief told him about the offer he had made to Mrs. Lee, and how she had answered it.", "M. Du Pin had a few private doubts as to how long a man's honorable resolution to leave the question with the lady herself would hold. He opened a negotiation for her ransom on the spot, and worked so effectually on the good feelings of Black Partridge that the chief agreed to bring his fair prisoner to Chicago at once, so that she could be restored to her friends.", "Whether the kind trader had anything in his mind at the outset beyond sympathy and brotherly kindness, we cannot say. We only know that in the course of time Mrs. Lee became Madame Du Pin, and that the worthy couple lived together in great happiness for many years afterward.", "The fate of Nau-non-gee, one of the chiefs of the Calumet village, mentioned earlier in this narrative, deserves to be recorded.", "During the battle of the 15th of August the man he went after, above all others, was one Sergeant Hays — a man from whom he had received many acts of kindness.", "Hays had already taken a ball through the body when the chief ran up to tomahawk him. The sergeant gathered what strength he had left and put his bayonet through him. They went down together. Other Indians came up and soon finished Hays, and only then was his bayonet drawn out of the body of his adversary.", "The wounded chief was carried after the battle to his village on the Calumet, and he lasted several days there. When he felt the end coming he called his young men together and charged them, in the most solemn terms he had, to see to the safety of the prisoners after his death and to take no life among them out of respect for his memory — since he had deserved what he got, at the hands of people whose kindness he had repaid so badly."],
  p2s24: ["Detroit had surrendered the day after the massacre at Chicago, and one of General Hull's stipulations in the surrender was that the inhabitants should be left undisturbed in their homes. So the family of Mr. Kinzie took up their quarters with friends in the old mansion — the one many people will still remember standing on the northwest corner of Jefferson Avenue and Wayne Street.", "They had a window on the winter that followed, and it worked on them. Almost every day there was something new to watch: American prisoners brought in by their Indian captors, and the cruelties practised on them. Men who could barely drag their wounded, bleeding feet over the frozen ground were made to dance, for their captors' amusement. Some of these exhibitions took place directly in front of the Government House, where Colonel McKee lived. British officers watched from their windows while it went on. For the honor of humanity we will hope that such instances were rare.", "Everything that could be turned into money or trade among the citizens' effects was offered, to buy their countrymen out of those hands. Then came the prisoners from the River Raisin — the men who, after surrendering to General Proctor, were handed to his savage allies to be tortured and murdered by inches — and the whole community moved at once. Private houses became hospitals. Everybody wanted to get hold of as many of the survivors as they could. The ladies of Detroit stood at their doors and windows watching the miserable creatures carried past for sale, and bartered away the very clothes they owned to get them.", "In Mr. Kinzie's house one large room was given over to the sufferers. Few of them lived. The two people spoken of afterward with the deepest feeling were young gentlemen from Kentucky, brothers, both badly wounded, both wounds made mortal by the treatment and hardship that followed. Each of them cared for nothing but the other. What they showed of that tender fraternal affection, in a hundred small ways, left an impression on that house that nobody in it ever lost.", "The last bargain of the winter was made by black Jim and one of the children, who were given leave to redeem a negro servant of the gallant Colonel Allen. The price was an old white horse. It was the only article of any value left in their possession.", "A brother of Colonel Allen came to Detroit afterward, and the negro chose to go back into servitude rather than stay on as a stranger in a strange land."],
  p2s25: ["Mr. Kinzie joined his family at Detroit in January, as has been told. Not long after, a suspicion took hold in General Proctor's mind: that he was in correspondence with General Harrison, who was at Fort Meigs and was believed to be planning an advance on Detroit. What happened next was that Lieutenant Watson of the British army called on him one day with an invitation to the General's quarters across the river — a matter of business, he wished to speak with him. Mr. Kinzie suspected nothing and went. As soon as he arrived he was ordered into confinement and put under strict guard in the house of his own former partner, Mr. Patterson of Sandwich. When he did not come home, Mrs. Kinzie sent word to some of the Indian chiefs who were his particular friends. They went straight to the commanding officer's head-quarters, demanded that \"their friend\" be released, and brought him back to his own house. The General waited. When a favorable opportunity came he sent a detachment of dragoons to arrest him, and this time they got him away and out onto the river. And at that moment a party of friendly Indians appeared.", "\"Where is the Shaw-nee-aw-kee?\" was the first thing they asked.", "\"There,\" his wife said, pointing across the water. \"In the hands of the red-coats, who are taking him away again.\"", "They ran to the river, seized what canoes were lying there, crossed to Sandwich, and compelled General Proctor a second time to give up what he intended.", "The third time, he managed it. Mr. Kinzie was arrested and carried in heavy irons to Fort Malden, in Canada, at the mouth of the Detroit River. At first they used him harshly. Later the rigor of his confinement slackened somewhat, and he was allowed to walk along the bank of the river for air and exercise."],
  p2s26: ["On the 10th of September he was out at his promenade under the close watch of his guard of soldiers, when a sound stopped the whole party where they stood: guns, out on Lake Erie, and not far below. What was there to make of it? Commodore Barclay must be firing on some of the Yankees. The guns went on. The time allowed the prisoner for his daily walk ran out, and neither he nor the men set over him took any notice that it had, because by then they were listening — every one of them certain now that ships of war were engaged out there. At last somebody reminded Mr. Kinzie that the hour had come for him to go back into confinement. He asked for another half-hour.", "\"Let me stay,\" he said, \"till we can learn how the battle has gone.\"", "Then a sloop came round the point under a press of sail, and behind her, giving chase, two gun-boats.", "\"She is running - she bears the British colors,\" cried he - \"yes, yes, they are lowering - she is striking her flag! Now,\" turning to the soldiers, \"I will go back to prison contented - I know how the battle has gone.\"", "That sloop was the Little Belt. She was the last vessel of the squadron to be taken by the gallant Perry on that memorable day, the one he reported afterward in words that will not be forgotten:", "\"We have met the enemy, and they are ours!\""],
  p2s27: ["Matters were growing critical, and the prisoners had to be moved somewhere safer than the frontier was now likely to be. It was resolved to send Mr. Kinzie to the mother-country. Nothing has ever come to light that explains General Proctor's course toward this gentleman. He had been taken out of the middle of his own family, where he was living quietly on the parole he had been given and under the protection of the stipulations of the surrender. He had been held for months. Now they set him on a horse under a strong guard, and the guard told him plainly that their orders were to shoot him through the head if he offered to speak to a single person on the road. They tied him onto the saddle so he could not get off it. Then they started for Quebec. One small thing happened on that road, and it illustrates as well as anything the course invariably pursued toward our citizens at that period by the British army on the Northwestern frontier.", "The saddle had not been properly fastened. The animal's motion was rough, and the saddle turned under him, and it twisted him into a position that was awkward and painful. His limbs were tied. He could not free himself, and he could not get down. The men in charge of him made him ride that way until he was nearly exhausted before they found the humanity to let him loose.", "At Quebec they put him aboard a small vessel bound for England. A few days out she was chased by an American frigate and driven into Halifax. She sailed a second time, sprang a leak, and had to put back.", "The attempt to send him across the ocean was given up, and he was returned to Quebec. What came next is as inexplicable as the arrest that started it: he was released. So was Mr. Macomb of Detroit, who had also been confined at Quebec, and both men were given permission to return to their friends and families, although the war was not over. It is possible that in their treatment the British commander-in-chief had been sheltering behind the plea that they were \"native-born British subjects,\" and that when it was learned Mr. Kinzie was in fact a citizen of the United States, letting him go looked like the safest course.", "Meanwhile General Harrison had reached Detroit at the head of his troops. He landed on the 29th of September. The whole town went out to meet him, and Mrs. Kinzie was among them, leading her children by the hand. The General walked her home and took up his quarters in her house. Kee-po-tah happened to be visiting the family just then, and the General was introduced to him. The two had met before: he had seen the chief the year previous, at the Council at Vincennes, and the meeting between them was one of great cordiality and interest."],
  p2s28: ["In 1816 Mr. Kinzie brought his family back to Chicago once more. A new fort went up on the old ground, somewhat larger than the one it replaced. The bones of the Americans massacred four years earlier were not gathered up and buried until after the troops came back.", "An Indian Agency was established there under the charge of Charles Jewett, Esq., of Kentucky. In 1820 Dr. Alexander Wolcott, of Connecticut, took his place, and he held the office until he died in 1830.", "The garrison was emptied of troops in 1823 and filled again in 1828, after the Winnebago war. That trouble had broken out between the Winnebagoes and the white settlers on and near the Mississippi. Murders were done, and the young chief Red Bird was taken and held at Prairie du Chien to wait for his trial, and there he killed himself — the chagrin of it, and the confinement, which he could not bear. There was real fear that the Pottowattamies would throw in with the Winnebagoes and set the whole frontier alight. What most likely stopped it was the work of Billy Caldwell, Robinson, and Shaw-bee-nay, who went out among the Rock River bands and reasoned with them, band by band, until they agreed to stay quiet.", "Chicago in those years held very few citizens, and their days went by much alike, one after another. Game was everywhere for the taking and the ground they broke was so rich that garden, corn-field, and dairy gave them more of every good thing than they could use. A friend back in the East countrie once put the obvious question to them.", "\"How do you dispose of all the good things you raise? You have no market?\" \"No.\" \"And you can't consume them all yourselves?\" \"No.\" \"What then do you do with them?\"", "\"Why, we manage, when a vessel arrives, to persuade the captain to accept a few kegs of butter, and stores of corn and vegetables, as a present, and that helps us to get rid of some of our overplus.\"", "Mail came in, as anyone would guess, only at long and uncertain intervals. Sometimes it came up from Fort Clark, at Peoria; more often from Fort Wayne, or else across the peninsula of Michigan, which was in those days a wilderness with savages living in it. The hardy man who carried it had more than once to do as the birds of heaven do and lodge among the branches, that being the only way to keep himself and his charge safe through the night.", "Visitors were scarce. Now and then a friend would come and stay for months at a stretch and take up the life of the woods along with them. Now and then a traveler on his way to or from parts unknown would turn up at the door, and a traveler could count on a warm and open-handed welcome.", "One such gentleman, coming north from the southern settlements, reached Wolf Point late in the evening. There was a small trading-establishment there then, kept by George Hunt and a Mr. Wallace. He stopped and asked whether they could put him and his horse up for the night. They told him they were poorly fitted to entertain a stranger — the house was small, and they were keeping bachelor's hall.", "\"Is there no place,\" asked the traveler, \"where I can obtain a lodging?\"", "\"Oh, yes - you will find a very comfortable house, Mr. Kinzie's, about half a mile below, near the mouth of the river.\"", "He pulled his horse's head around and rode the way they had pointed.", "At the house, the first thing he asked was, \"Is this the residence of Mr. Kinzie?\"", "\"Yes, sir.\"", "\"I should be glad to get accommodation for myself and horse.\"", "\"Certainly, sir - walk in.\"", "Someone led the horse off to the stable and the gentleman was shown into a parlor where two ladies were sitting. They went through the usual opening questions and answers, people in a new country getting acquainted quickly, and before very long he was sitting down to a hot supper, and we may safely call it a fine one, the table in that house having always had a certain reputation.", "He appeared to think well of it, because he settled in thoroughly. After tea he called for a boot-jack and pulled off his boots. The ladies were somewhat taken aback. But they had been living a long while outside the world, and they could not be sure what etiquette had been doing in their absence.", "Before he went up for the night he let them know what he would like for his breakfast, and his breakfast was made accordingly. The next day came on stormy. He was content where he was, and having first satisfied himself that his horse wanted for nothing and was in no way neglected, he passed the day quite comfortably.", "When he tired of his book he would talk a while with the family. He showed no inclination to keep himself apart. He unbent, in fact, into a decent sociability, considering they were all of them out there in the woods together.", "On the second day it cleared. The traveler announced that he would be going. He ordered his horse brought around to the door, and then he asked for his bill.", "\"My house isn't a tavern, sir,\" was the astounding reply.", "\"Not a tavern! Good heavens! have I been making myself at home in this manner in a private family?\"", "He apologized at length and there was no need of a word of it. The family had understood his mistake from the moment he walked in, and had spent the whole of his visit privately enjoying the thought of his face when he finally found out.", "It was in that same year, 1816 — the year the fort was raised again after the Indians destroyed it — that the Pottowattamies ceded to the United States the tract of land Chicago stands on, and the country around it. For twenty years more they went on living there peaceably. Not until 1836 did the Government remove them to the lands set aside for their use on the Upper Missouri.", "In 1830 Commissioners appointed by the State came and laid the town of Chicago out into lots. A lot cost, in those days, somewhere between ten and sixty dollars.", "Mr. Kinzie had always said what this place would become. He knew where it sat on the map and he knew the richness of the country behind it, and he never doubted that prosperity and importance were coming to it. He was not allowed to see any of it proved. He closed his useful and energetic life on the 6th of January, 1828, having just finished his sixty-fifth year."],
  p2s29: ["Chicago was not, when I first saw it, the cheerful and happy place it had been. Dr. Wolcott had died, and Lieutenant Furman, and a promising young son of Mr. Beaubien, the three of them within a few weeks of one another, and every branch of the little social circle lay under the gloom of it.", "The season's weather was worse than anything anyone there could remember. In two months there were exactly two days on which the sun stayed out from morning to night. Even in the second week of April, when my husband set off to go back to Fort Winnebago, the storms were bad enough to drive him and his men into an Indian lodge, where they had to lie by for two or three days.", "Robert Kinzie had gone off to the Calumet at that same hour to hunt, and Medard Beaubien and Billy Caldwell with him. Day after day passed with no word of them, and we made up our minds they had frozen to death. They came back at last, to our infinite joy. What had saved them was that Robert and Caldwell had each thought to take two blankets instead of one.", "Riding was our one recreation, when the weather permitted it — through the woods that lie north of the river, or south across the prairie along the shore of the lake.", "Going north, a little bridle-path took us along the line of what is now Rush Street. The boughs met thickly overhead the whole way, and often enough we had to break the shrubs back out of the path as we rode. We generally turned around at the little prairie that lies west of Wright's Woods.", "Going south, we nearly always came on a new-comer, Dr. Harmon, standing over the building of a sod fence on a piece of ground he had chosen near the lake shore. As the season came on he took to planting fruit-stones of every description inside the inclosure, laying down a garden and an orchard against the years to come.", "We would stop and talk a while. He had two subjects — horticulture, and the certainty that Chicago was going to matter. That it would one day be a great city he never doubted for a moment; and by that time, forest and prairie though the whole of it was, we had half come round to believing it ourselves.", "When the afternoons turned pleasant, later in the season, we took up pistol-firing, which entertained us a good deal. It was done outside the pickets, with the mark set on a panel of one of the bastions. The gentlemen must forgive me for putting it on record, but the ladies in time got good enough to carry off the honors as a rule. One of them, Mrs. Hunter, was a great shot; she had brought down her grouse once on the wing, much to the delight of an officer named Captain Martin Scott, of raccoon celebrity.", "Every so often the fort had a small sensation of its own. A settler would be found out selling the soldiers milk-punch under the name of milk, which interfered in no small degree with the regularity and the perfect discipline of the service. The first step was to drum the offender out with all the honors of war — a party-colored suit on his back and the Rogue's March playing behind him. The second was to shut up everyone he had imposed upon in the guard-house, where the lady of the commanding officer fed them coffee and hot cakes in quantity, so that their eyes might be opened to the enormity of what they had done. It is no wonder the officers complained now and then that the men competed to get into the guard-house rather than to stay out. They knew, the poor fellows, when they were well off.", "We were rowed up to Wolf Point one Sunday to attend a religious service, held by a man everyone called Father See.", "He was tall and thin, in a green frock-coat, and out of its sleeves hung a pair of hands that gave abundant evidence, as did the rest of his dress, that he had no great opinion of the axiom - \"cleanliness is a part of holiness.\"", "He stepped briskly up behind a table on a little platform and began. His subject was \"The fear of God.\"", "\"There was a kind of fear,\" he told us, \"that was very nearly a_lee_-a-nated to love: so nearly, that it wasn't worth while splitting hairs for the difference.\" Then he set about describing that kind of fear. The description tangled as he went, and tangled further, until he stopped altogether, thoroughly lost, and said, \"Come, let's stop a little while, and clear away the brush.\" He picked apart the thread of his ideas as well as he could and started off again. Before long he had lost his way a second time and came to another halt. He mopped the perspiration off his forehead with a red cotton handkerchief that was many degrees from clean, and said, \"Now, suppose we drive back a little piece.\" So he went back over what he wished to impress upon us: that we must cherish the fear that maketh wise unto salvation, \"which fear,\" he said, \"may we all enjoy, that together we may soar away, on the rolling clouds of aether, to a boundless and happy eternity, which is the wish of your humble servant.\" Then he flung his hands wide, made the best of dancing-school bows, and sat down.", "We came away satisfied that our own religious exercises at home were the more edifying of the two, and we kept to them after that.", "Our brother Robert Kinzie came back from Palestine — the seat of the Land Office, not the Holy Land — carrying the certificate of the family's title to the piece of Chicago that has since been called \"Kinzie's Addition.\" We took it as the making of a home for us some day, if good Dr. Harmon and a few others should turn out to be right in their glorious dreams. One small incident will show how modest the expectations of most people were at that period.", "The certificate stood in Robert's name, since it was he who had applied on the family's behalf. What it described was a fractional quarter-section — one hundred and two acres, not one hundred and sixty, because the river and Lake Michigan cut fifty-eight acres off the southern and the eastern lines of it. An applicant in that case was at liberty to take his missing fifty-eight acres wherever he liked, out of whatever land had not been appropriated already.", "\"Now, my son,\" said his mother to Robert, \"lay your claim on the corn-field at Wolf Point. It is fine land, and will always be valuable for cultivation, besides, as it faces down the main river, the situation will always be a convenient one.\"", "Robert laughed out loud at her. \"Hear mother!\" he said. \"We have just got a hundred and two acres - more than we shall ever want, or know what to do with, and now she would have me go and claim fifty-eight acres more!\"", "\"Take my advice, my boy,\" his mother said again, \"or you may live one day to regret it.\"", "\"Well, I can't see how I can ever regret not getting more than we can possibly use.\" That closed the matter. Nobody ever claimed the fifty-eight acres, and I think there was a general feeling that to ask for what was ours by right would have had a grasping, covetous look about it. Five-and-twenty years have made us a great deal wiser.", "I stayed two months at Chicago, and our mother spent many of those evenings telling me about her early life and the adventures in it. What follows is her account of her captivity among the Senecas. I have set it down in the form of a tale, but with not the slightest variation from the facts as she gave them to me, and from her sister — the little Maggie of the story — Mrs. William Forsyth, of Sandwich (C.W.)."],
  p2s30: ["Before the war of the Revolution the whole western portion of Pennsylvania belonged to the Indian tribes, and everybody knows it. Among them the Delawares were friends to the whites, and when the great struggle opened they took the part of the United States. The Iroquois were the friends and allies of the mother-country.", "Very few white settlers had ventured past the Susquehanna. The country beyond was crossed by roving bands — Shawanoes, Nanticokes and others — who at times professed friendship with the Americans and acted in concert with the Delawares, or Lenape, as allies, and at other times let themselves be worked upon by their neighbors the Iroquois until they showed the most sanguinary spirit of hostility.", "That was the arithmetic of life on the frontier: constant peril, constant alarm. Many a scene of dismal barbarity was acted out there, as the histories of the time will testify, and even the people who felt themselves in some measure protected by their immediate neighbors the Delawares never for a day forgot the caution their exposed situation demanded of them.", "The military garrison at Pittsburg — Fort Pitt, as it was then called — gave some added security to those who had pushed farther west into the fertile valleys of the Alleghany and the Monongahela. Among these was the family of Mr. Lytle, who some years before the opening of our story had moved from Baltimore to Path Valley, near Carlisle, and afterward settled himself on the banks of Plum River, a tributary of the Alleghany. There he lived with his wife and five children, comfortable and secure, never once visited by an enemy, and troubled only by the occasional false alarm raised by his more timorous neighbors — people with longer experience of frontier life, who were prone to anticipate evil and to magnify every appearance of danger.", "On a bright afternoon in the autumn of 1779, two of Mr. Lytle's children were playing in a little dingle behind their father's house — a girl of nine and her brother, two years younger. Some large trees had been felled there not long before and were still lying about untrimmed, their branches on them, and there were logs cut for fuel scattered over the ground. The children tired of their game and sat down on one of the logs, and to pass the time they got to talking about a thing that had been troubling them both.", "A few hours earlier, playing in the same place, they thought they had seen an Indian crouched behind one of the fallen trees. The Indians of the neighborhood paid occasional visits to the family, and the children knew many of them well and were fond of them. This one they did not know. After the first look they ran for the house.", "Their mother scolded them for the story they brought in, and set about proving it had nothing in it. \"You know,\" she said, \"you are always alarming us unnecessarily: the neighbors' children have frightened you to death. Go back to your play, and learn to be more courageous.\"", "So they went back to their sports, not much persuaded by their mother's arguments. And while they were sitting there on the tree-trunk, something interrupted them. It sounded like a quail, not far off.", "\"Listen,\" said the boy. A second note answered the first. \"Do you hear that?\"", "\"Yes,\" she said. Then, after a moment: \"Do you not hear a rustling among the branches of the tree yonder?\"", "\"Maybe it is a squirrel — but look! what is that? Surely I saw something red among the branches. It looked like a fawn popping up its head.\"", "They were staring so hard at the fallen tree that they had forgotten there was any other direction. Hands took them from behind. They were pinioned in a grip like iron, and they were in the arms of savages, whose faces and gestures told them plainly enough that these were enemies.", "The men made signs that meant silence, on pain of death, and hurried them off, half dead with terror, on a course leading away from their father's house. They went a long way without a word spoken. As night came on the severity of their captors slackened a little, and the party halted and took the usual precautions against being surprised.", "Torn out of their home, taken from their parents, and knowing every horror that the rumors of that time attached to a captivity among the Indians — a torturing death, perhaps, at the end of it — the children could hold it in no longer, and gave way to sobbing and lamentation.", "Their distress seemed to touch one of the party, a man of mild aspect, who came over and tried to quiet them. He made them a couch out of the long grass that grew near the encamping-place. He gave them a share of his own stock of dried meat and parched corn. And he made them understand, by signs, that no further harm was intended them."],
  p2s31: ["The kindness lasted exactly as long as it took a second party of the enemy to come up. They had the mother of the little prisoners with them, and in her arms was the youngest of the family, an infant three months old.", "Their father had gone off early that day with the serving-men to a raising a few miles away, and the house was left without a defender in it. Nobody had thought twice about that. The country had been quiet for a long stretch of years — nothing to molest anyone, nothing to raise an alarm — and quiet gets into a household and softens it. Lately they had set aside some of the caution they had once thought necessary.", "The party had lain in wait for the favorable moment, and when it came they took the defenceless family prisoner. Afterward Mrs. Lytle studied the paint on their faces, and the other marks by which the early settlers learned to tell one tribe from another, and concluded that she and her children had fallen into the hands of Senecas. She was not mistaken. They had come down from their village meaning to fall on some isolated band of Delawares, who were their enemies; they had failed to find them; and they had made up the loss by carrying off a few white settlers instead.", "That the tribe was, on the whole, a mild one, and that the chief with this party was a man of some magnanimity — those two facts, and nothing else, are why the Lytles did not end as most Americans ended who fell into the hands of the Iroquois.", "From their mother the children learned what she did not know. She had no word at all of the other two, the boy of six and the little girl of four. The servant-girl had disappeared at the very beginning as well, and the mother let herself hope that the three of them had got away together.", "They halted a few hours, long enough for the prisoners' exhausted bodies to recover a little, and then the march began again. One of the older men offered to take the infant off her hands; she had carried it in her arms until then. The kindness was more than she had looked for, and she gave the child up to him.", "They went on. The man carrying the infant let himself fall back a little from the rest. When he came to a spot that suited his purpose he took the child by the feet and swung it once, the whole way round, to put his weight behind the blow, and broke its head against a tree. He left the body where it lay and rejoined the party.", "The mother suspected nothing of what had happened. She watched him come up again with his arms empty, and looked at him hard, and then looked wildly round at the rest of the group. Her beloved little one was not there. The absence said what had become of it. She had the shriek in her and she held it down, because she knew that the lives of the ones still with her depended on her firmness in that hour, and she drew them closer against her and went on along that melancholy road without a word spoken or a question asked.", "Out of the depths of her heart she cried to Him who is able to save, and He comforted her with hope of deliverance for the ones who were left; because she could see that if blood had been the whole object of these men, her scalp and her children's would have been taken in the yard where they were seized.", "And there was the one who was plainly in command of them. In his eyes she read something more merciful than she had dared to hope for. She had particularly noticed how soothing his manner was, and how openly he favored her eldest — the little girl of whom we have spoken — and on those slender foundations she built one bright hope after another of escape, or of ransom."],
  p2s33: ["The march went on for days, and every day of it cost them something. At the end of it the party came into the Seneca village on the head-waters of the Alleghany, near the place now called Olean Point. The chief who had brought them there was known among his people as the Big White Man. He walked his prisoners up to the principal lodge, which belonged to his mother, widow of the head-chief of that band. They called her the Old Queen.", "He brought the little girl forward into his mother's presence and presented her.", "\"My mother,\" he said, \"I bring you a child to supply the place of my brother, who was killed by the Lenape six moons ago. She shall dwell in my lodge, and be to me a sister. Take the white woman and her children and treat them kindly — our father will give us many horses and guns to buy them back again.\"", "By their father he meant the British Indian Agent of his tribe, Colonel Johnson, a good and benevolent gentleman who lived at Fort Niagara on the British side of the river of that name. The old queen did exactly as her son had told her. She took the prisoners in, and everything her simple and primitive way of living could provide for their comfort, she provided."],
  p2s32: ["We must go back now to the place and the hour where this story began.", "Late in the evening of that same day the father came home. The house was silent, and so was everything around it. Nothing living was left anywhere on the place, indoors or out. His nearest neighbors lived a long way off. He went to them at a run and demanded tidings of his family from people still climbing out of sleep.", "One and another of them joined the search, and at the house of one of them the servant-maid was found. She had got away. Her first refuge, she said, had been a large brewing-tub in the outer kitchen, and she had got under it at the first alarm and stayed there. The Indians were plainly in a hurry. Their going gave her the chance to slip out and make for safety. Of her mistress and the children she could tell him nothing, except this: they had not been murdered where she could see it or hear it.", "They quartered the neighborhood all night and turned up nothing. Then Mr. Lytle remembered an old settler who lived alone far up the valley, and they went straight there. The man had been at work in his field just before sunset, he said, and a party of strange Indians had passed close by his cabin. He had watched them wind along the brow of the hill. They had prisoners with them — a woman and a child. He knew the woman was white, he said, because she carried her infant in her arms, and not on her back, in the manner of the savages.", "Day was breaking by then. The whole night had gone in searching and nothing had come of it. The father talked it over with the neighbors who had stayed with him, and when they offered to ride with him to Fort Pitt and lay the case before the commandant and the Indian Agent, he took the offer.", "Going down the valley they came in sight of a hut they had searched in the dark and found empty. Two children were standing on the high bank in front of it, and the sight of them stopped the party where it stood. The father knew them at once for two of his missing flock. They could tell him nothing about their mother or the others. What they could tell him was simple, and they told it plainly.", "They had been playing in the garden when they saw Indians come into the yard by the house. Nobody looked their way. The brother, who was six, got his little sister over the fence into a field grown thick with blackberry and wild raspberry, and they lay down in it. They stayed hidden there a while, and then, finding everything quiet, they began working toward the far side of the field, away from the house. The girl had pulled off her shoes and stockings in the garden, at play, and the briers went into her bare feet with every step. She was very close to crying out. Her brother took off his own stockings and put them on her, and then tried his shoes on her too, but they were too big and kept coming off, and she could not walk in them. They kept on. They believed they were escaping certain death, for, as I have said, the children of that country were raised on stories that taught them to see in every strange Indian a minister of torture, and of horrors worse than death. Then the little girl's strength ran out. Worn down by the pain and the walking, she said at last that she could go no farther.", "\"Then, Maggie,\" said her brother, \"I must kill you, for I can't let you be killed by the Indians.\"", "\"Oh, no, Thomas!\" she said. \"Don't, pray don't kill me! I don't think the Indians will find us.\"", "\"Oh, yes, they will, Maggie, and I could kill you so much easier than they would.\"", "He kept at it a long time, reasoning with her, and while he reasoned he was looking about him for a stick big enough for the purpose. Despair put some strength back into the little creature. She promised him she would not complain and would not falter, not once, if he would only help her get out of the field.", "That a boy of six could believe he was saving his sister from savage barbarity by killing her himself is the whole measure of what the children of the early settlers were told, and grew up believing.", "A few more efforts brought them out of the field and into an uninclosed pasture-ground, and there, to their great delight, were cows feeding. The children knew them at once. They belonged to Granny Myers, an old woman who lived at some little distance — though in which direction from where they were now standing, neither of them had the least idea.", "The boy worked it out with a sense beyond his years. \"Let us hide ourselves till sunset,\" he said, \"when the cows will go home, and we will follow them.\"", "They did. The cows took them home at sunset, and to their dismay the house stood deserted. Granny Myers had been called down the valley on some business and did not come back that night.", "They were too tired and too hungry to go anywhere else. They tried to get milk out of the cows and got almost none, and then they lay down to sleep under an old bedstead standing behind the house. In the night their father and his party gave them one more terror. Men were shouting and calling, and the children, who could not tell friends from foes, took the noise for the whoop of Indians and pressed themselves together as far out of sight as they could get. The shouts had only been meant to rouse the people of the house. In the morning, when he found them, they were debating between themselves what course to take next for safety."],
  p2s34: ["The commandant at Fort Pitt took up Mr. Lytle's business as if it were his own. He gave him a detachment of soldiers to go with him and his friends after the marauders. Certain circumstances had already turned suspicion toward the Senecas, and it was among the villages of that tribe that the search settled.", "They asked their questions in a dozen directions and asked them carefully, every time, because all the tribes of the Iroquois — the Five Nations, as they grandly called themselves — were allies of Great Britain and carried a settled hatred of the Americans. So it was a long while before the father and his party stood at last in the village of the Big White Man.", "A treaty for the ransom of the captives was opened at once, and for Mrs. Lytle and the younger child it was easily done. For the little Eleanor, the adopted child of the tribe, there was no price. Nothing moved him — not offers, not entreaties, not promises. \"No,\" the chief said, \"she was his sister, he had taken her to supply the place of his brother who was killed by the enemy — she was dear to him, and he wouldn't part with her.\"", "Nothing the father tried would shake that resolution. In the end he had to go, and take with him only such of his beloved ones as he had had the good fortune to recover.", "We will not try to set down what such parents feel, made to give up a darling child and leave her among people they had had every reason, until then, to think merciless. There was no alternative. They commended her to the care of their heavenly Father, and they took some comfort — real comfort — from the plain tenderness with which she had been treated so far, and on that they started the melancholy trip home, telling themselves that the next attempt would be the one that brought her back.", "He put his family somewhere safe at Pittsburg first. Then, still backed by the commandant and the Indian Agent, he set out for the frontier and the house of the British Agent, Colonel Johnson. He laid the whole case out, and it went straight to that benevolent officer's heart. Johnson promised to spare no exertion on his behalf, and he kept the promise to the letter. As soon as the spring opened the country he went in person to the village of the Big White Man and offered him splendid presents, guns and horses both. The chief was inexorable."],
  p2s35: ["Time went on, and each year took a little more out of the hope of getting the little captive back. She, meanwhile, was winding herself closer and closer around the heart of her Indian brother. Nothing could exceed the consideration and affection she was shown, and not by him alone — his mother, the Old Queen, was the same. Every brooch and every string of wampum the lodge owned went onto the child. The principal seat was hers, and the most delicate food was set in front of her first, every time, without discussion. Nothing was left undone that might make her happy, or make her forget the home and the people she came from.", "She had watched her parents and her dear little brother go, and felt something close to despair, and for a long time afterward she would not be consoled by anybody. She would rather have died than live apart from everyone she loved. But time does what it always does. It brought its soothing balm, and in the end she was contented, and then happy.", "She was quick, and she never stopped moving, and the energy in her was the kind that stayed remarkable in her to the last period of her life. It earned her a name. They called her The Ship under full sail."],
  p2s36: ["There was one drawback to the little prisoner's happiness, apart from her longing after her own dear home: the wife of the Big White Man hated her. She had hated her from the day the child arrived and was taken into the family as a sister, and at first she had the prudence to keep it out of her husband's sight.", "There was nothing strange in a wife feeling some jealousy at watching her own place in her husband's heart taken by the child of their enemy, the American. It might have stopped there. It did not, because the woman had a bad and vindictive temper to feed it, and because her husband met her complaints and her murmurings with indifference.", "She had no children of her own to take up her attention. So her mind closed on the wrongs she believed had been done her, and then on what might be done about them, and it worked at that day after day. She did not have to wait long for her chance.", "The Big White Man went off with a party — a war-party or a hunting-excursion, it made no difference — and while he was gone his little sister came down with fever and ague. The Old Queen nursed her with the utmost tenderness. So, to all appearances, did the chief's wife, who was tireless in her attentions to the little favorite, and who understood that this was the surest way to keep anyone from looking at her twice.", "One afternoon, while the Old Queen was out of the lodge, her daughter-in-law came in carrying a bowl of something she had made. She crouched down beside the mat where the child was lying and spoke to her in an affectionate voice. \"Drink, my sister. I have brought you that which will drive this fever far from you.\"", "The little girl lifted her head to answer, and as she did she saw, through a crevice in the wall of the lodge, a pair of eyes. They were fixed on her, and there was something in them, some meaning being pushed at her very hard. She had a quick natural understanding, sharpened by her time among these people, and she used it. \"Set it down, my sister,\" she said faintly. \"When this fit of the fever has passed, I will drink your medicine.\"", "The squaw was too careful to press her. She moved about the lodge a while longer, doing nothing in particular, and then went out to another lodge close by. The bright eyes stayed at the crevice. They followed the woman all the way out of sight. Then a low voice spoke — a young voice, a friend, one of the children she played with.", "\"Don't drink that which your brother's wife has brought you. She hates you, and is only waiting an opportunity to rid herself of you. I have watched her all the morning, and have seen her gathering the most deadly roots and herbs. I knew for whom they were intended, and came here to warn you.\"", "\"Take the bowl,\" said the little invalid, \"and carry it to my mother's lodge.\"", "It was carried there, and it was examined. What was in it was chiefly a decoction of the root of the May-apple, which is the deadliest poison known among the Indians.", "There are no words for what went through that small community when the thing became known. The squaws ran to and fro, the way they do when they are roused, each of them trying to outdo the last in what she called down on the culprit's head. No punishment beyond that was inflicted on her for the present. When the first burst of rage had spent itself, she was met with a silent abhorrence instead.", "The little patient was moved into the lodge of the Old Queen and kept under guard. Her enemy was left to walk by herself through the fields and the woods, in silence and solitude, waiting for her husband to come home and decide what would be done with her.", "In a few days the excursion ended and the Big White Man came back to the village with his party. What he learned there was that someone had tried to kill his little sister. Contrary to the usual custom of savages, he took no summary vengeance in that first transport. He banished the woman from his lodge, never to return, and he condemned her to hoe corn in a far corner of the great field that served the whole community for a garden.", "The vindictiveness in her never went out. Whenever the little girl and her companions strayed near that part of the field she would show it — a swing of the hoe at the child, or some other spiteful thing — but either she was watched too closely, or she stood too much in awe of the man who had been her husband, and she never made a second attempt on his sister's life."],
  p2s37: ["Four years had gone by since little Nelly was carried off. Her heart was warm and affectionate by nature, and warmth of that kind answers what it is given; the people she lived among had been unstinting with her, and she had paid them back in the same coin. She loved the chief and his mother, and something in her feeling for them went past love into reverence. She had taken up their language and their customs so completely that she had all but lost her own.", "She belonged to the tribe now. Her old home and her own family had gone dim in her — all of it except her mother. Her mother had not faded at all. That love had been fierce in her from the beginning, as everything in her was fierce, and no amount of time or change was ever going to touch it.", "Then came the peace of 1783 between Great Britain and the United States. A general pacification of the Indian tribes followed it, and in the hearts of Mr. and Mrs. Lytle the old hope stood up again.", "They moved the whole family to Fort Niagara. Not far from it, on the American side, burned the Great Council-Fire of the Senecas. Colonel Johnson took up the negotiation again willingly, and, to leave no chance of success unused, he went once more in person to the village of the Big White Man.", "He could not have chosen a better week. He arrived in the middle of the Feast of the Green Corn — an observance so like the Jewish Feast of Tabernacles that it has helped persuade many people, along with other of their customs, that the Indian nations are what became of the lost ten tribes of Israel. Everything else in the village stopped for it. The people gave the days over to one another, out in the open air or under arbors built of green branches, and everyone came out in gala-dress. The little adopted child wore a petticoat of blue broadcloth bordered with gay-colored ribbons and, over it, a sack of black silk carrying three rows of silver brooches — the center row large, running from her throat to the hem, the rows from either shoulder no bigger than a shilling-piece and set as close together as they would go. Around her neck were more strings of white and purple wampum than anyone could have counted, worked out of the inner surface of the muscle-shell. Her hair was clubbed behind and heavy with beads of every color. Scarlet cloth leggings, and moccasins of deer-skin embroidered with porcupine-quills, finished her.", "Colonel Johnson was received with every consideration owed to his position, and to the long friendship between him and that tribe.", "He could see that the festival had warmed and opened all of them, and he used it. In an interview with the chief he spoke about what the child's father and mother had done for love of her — given up their friends, given up their home, come hundreds of miles into this country on the single hope of one day looking at her and putting their arms around her. The chief listened, and the chief softened. He would come to the Grand Council soon to be held at Fort Niagara, on the British side of the river, he said, and he would bring his little sister with him.", "He wanted something in return, and he named it plainly. Not only was no effort to be made to reclaim the child; no proposal to part with her was to be put to him at all.", "The day came, and Nelly was lifted onto a horse to ride with her Indian brother to the Great Council of the Senecas, and her heart went the whole way at a gallop. She had given him her promise, long before, that she would never leave him without his permission. He had her word, and he rode easy on it.", "Band after band of chiefs and warriors came in to meet their Father, the agent, at the council-fire — and with every one of them the parents' hearts went up and came down again. The officers of the fort had given them quarters for as long as this took. The ladies of the garrison, who had taken the whole business to heart, walked out with the mother to the place of council and stood there with her, watching for the first sign of the band from the Alleghany River.", "At last it was there, coming out of the forest on the opposite side, the American side. The commanding officer sent boats across for the chief and his party. The father and mother went down to the grassy bank with all the officers and ladies behind them and waited, and they had seen it in the first glance across the water: the little captive was with them.", "At the boat the chief stopped and spoke to some of his young men. \"Stand here with the horses, and wait until I return.\"", "He was told the horses could be ferried over with him and looked after on the other side.", "\"No,\" he said. \"Let them wait.\"", "He kept hold of his darling's hand as the boat went out. He held it the whole way across, and while the river went under them, and until the bow came against the bank — and until the child tore loose and ran forward into the arms of the mother she had been taken from.", "The chief watched that happen, and he could hold out no longer.", "\"She shall go,\" he said. \"The mother must have her child again. I will go back alone.\"", "He made one silent gesture of farewell, turned, and stepped down into the boat. Nothing anyone said could get him to stay for the council — not argument, not entreaty. On the far bank he swung up onto his horse, and he and his young men were gone into the depths of the forest.", "They stayed a few weeks at Niagara. Then Mr. Lytle began to think about what a man's resolution is worth after the man has gone home alone, and about what might be done to take his child a second time, and he decided to move again. He crossed Lake Erie with his family at the first opportunity and settled near Detroit, and there he lived out the rest of his life.", "Little Nelly never saw her friend the chief again. She never forgot him either. To the day of her death she spoke of her brother the Big White Man with tenderness and gratitude, and of her friends and playfellows among the Senecas."],
  p2s38: ["The heroine of the story just told was fourteen years old when she married Colonel McKillip, an officer in the British service. He died in 1794, near the place afterward called Fort Defiance, at the Miami Rapids. A body of British troops had come down from Detroit to hold that post; General Wayne was in the field against the Indians at the time, and the British Government judged it proper to make a demonstration or two on behalf of its allies. The Colonel had ridden out with a party to reconnoiter. He was coming back in after dark when one of his own sentinels fired on him and killed him. Mrs. Helm was the child of that marriage.", "Widowed, Mrs. McKillip went to live with her parents at Grosse Pointe, eight miles up the river from Detroit. It was while she was there that the thing happened which she told over so many times afterward, always with a particular attention, because of how melancholy and how unaccountable it was.", "Of all that large family she loved her second brother, Thomas Lytle, best; he had the kindest and most affectionate nature of any of them. He was courting a young lady who lived at the river Trench,[43] which is what they called it then — it is the Thames now — a stream that runs into Lake St. Clair some twenty miles above Detroit. To go to her he would take his horse across the Detroit River on the ferry and then ride overland to the Trench, which at certain seasons could be forded.", "Late in the spring, on a fine forenoon, he said good-bye to his mother and his sister and set off on one of these visits. They generally kept him away two or three days.", "After dinner his sister was sitting with her work at an open window that looked into a small side inclosure planted with fruit-trees. Something came between her and the light. She looked up. Her brother Thomas was standing outside the window. His horse was not with him. He had his saddle up on his shoulders.", "She had heard no gate. That puzzled her, and so did the sight of him carrying his gear that way, and she called out to him and asked what had happened and why he was back so soon. He did not answer her. He only looked hard into her face, and went on along the paved walk that runs down to the stables, slowly.", "She sat where she was for a little while, waiting for him to come round and explain himself and his adventures. He did not come. At last she could not stand the waiting, and she laid her work aside and went through to the back of the house to look for him.", "Her mother was the first person she met. \"Have you seen Thomas?\" she asked.", "\"Thomas! He has gone to the river Trench.\"", "\"No, he has returned - I saw him pass the window not fifteen minutes since.\"", "\"Then he will be in soon.\"", "She could not wait for that. She went out to the stables and hunted everywhere, in every direction. There was no Thomas. There was no horse. There was no saddle. She asked the servants and not one of them had seen him. She went back into the house and told her mother what had happened.", "\"You must have fallen asleep and dreamed it,\" said her mother.", "\"No, indeed! I was wide awake - I spoke to him, and he gave me no answer, but such a look!\"", "The uneasiness stayed on her all afternoon, and no argument she made to herself would shift it.", "In the morning a messenger came from the river Trench with dismal news.", "They had found the young man and his horse a little way below the ford, both of them drowned.", "It appeared that when he reached the bank he found the river running much higher than usual, swollen with the recent rains. He would have to swim it with the horse. So he stripped off his clothes, tied them into a bundle, and made the bundle fast across his shoulders. What they believed, from how the bodies lay and how they looked when found, was that the force of the current shifted that bundle on his back, and that the weight of it pulled his head down under the water and held it there, and that he could not lift it again.", "He had been seen going past a house near the stream on his way to the ford, and the hour of that is known. Set it against the hour at the window, and there is no getting away from it: he died at the moment his sister saw, or believed she saw, him pass in front of her.", "When these sad particulars were told to me, I could not keep myself from asking, - \"Mother, is it not possible this might have been a dream?\"", "\"A dream? No, indeed, my child. I was perfectly wide awake - as much so as I am at this moment. I am not superstitious. I have never believed in ghosts or witches, but nothing can ever persuade me that this wasn't a warning sent from God, to prepare me for my brother's death.\"", "Anyone who knew her knew what she was: a woman of rational, level good sense, with no fancies and no fears in her, and a calm self-possession that never once left her in circumstances that would try anyone. Knowing that, you find yourself very nearly seeing the thing as she saw it.", "The order had now come for the evacuation of Fort Dearborn and the removal of the troops to Fort Howard, at Green Bay. That meant the family circle would be broken up. Our mother, and our sister Mrs. Helm with her little son, were to come back with us to Fort Winnebago. The rest of the family, Robert excepted, would go with the command to Green Bay.", "The schooner Napoleon was to come round from Detroit to carry the troops and all their goods and chattels to the post assigned them. Our own party would travel overland. We meant to pick a shorter and pleasanter road than the one that had brought us here. My husband had arrived with his Frenchmen, Petaille Grignon and Simon Lecuyer, and after that every pair of hands in the house was busy with the work of breaking up and getting away."],
  p2s39: ["The people at Hickory Creek were generous, and I would be treating them badly if I left out the party they threw for our Chicago beaux around this time. A ball, no less, and every one of Chicago's five bachelors was asked to it. Mr. Dole had only lately come into the country and sent his regrets. Lieutenant Foster had the duty and could not go, but what he did instead was worth rather more than his company: he handed his beautiful horse over to Medard Beaubien. Medard, with Robert Kinzie and Gholson Kercheval alongside him, was already counting on a splendid time of it — putting the local beaux entirely in the shade, and turning the head of every belle at Hickory Creek.", "Then as now, people spoke of Chicago as the City par excellence. Its handful of residents carried a reputation for having seen something of the world, and we may safely assume that three smart, dashing young men riding down was a prospect the girls of that small place welcomed rather more warmly than did the young men whose place they stood to take.", "The day arrived and off they went in the highest spirits, early enough to be certain of it, since the dancing was to open at two in the afternoon. Each of the three sat a horse he was proud of. Each had put on his best suit. That was only proper in city gallants who meant to leave their less fashionable neighbors nowhere at all, and to break as many admiring country hearts as came within reach.", "They arrived to find themselves treated with the greatest politeness. Somebody took charge of the steeds, a dinner appeared, and when they had eaten they were shown through into the dancing-hall.", "Beauty from every precinct round about had turned out for it. Most of the ladies were white, or near enough to pass for it, though a dash of copper color showed here and there among them. Bombazet gowns, and big white pocket-handkerchiefs that smelled of cinnamon oil — there was no shortage of either — and once they had lined themselves up in long rows down the puncheon floor, you could not have found a happier or a merrier company anywhere in the world.", "Then the evening began to lean. Hour by hour the city gentlemen grew more gallant; hour by hour the girls grew more pleased with the attention; and hour by hour the local swains went darker in the face. They pigeon-winged. They double-shuffled. They came near throwing hips and shoulders out of the socket at \"hoe corn and dig potatoes,\" and not one bit of it counted. They had to stand there and watch three sprigs from Chicago take their \"pick and choose\" among the girls they had thought of as their own, until they had been danced clean off their own floor.", "They had danced until daylight, and now it was time to be starting back. Nobody appeared, with any obliging politeness, to bring the horses out of the stable for them.", "\"Poor fellows!\" one of them said, with a pitying sort of laugh. \"They couldn't stand it. They have gone home to bed!\"", "\"Serves them right,\" another put in. \"They'd better not ask us down among their girls again!\"", "They groped their way over to the stable and stepped inside. Animals stood at the manger, right enough, but not the animals they had left there. So what were these? A trick of the rogues, then — strange nondescripts pushed in where their own horses belonged?", "They walked them out into the gray of the morning, and then they saw what they had.", "The bodies were their own bodies, no question of that. But the manes were gone. So were the tails. A scrubby, pickety ridge along the neck and a bare stump sticking out behind — that was every last remnant of the flowing splendor in which they had ridden down to \"bear away the bell\" at Hickory Creek, or, to say it the way the country says it, \"to take the rag off the bush.\"", "Gholson dropped onto a log and wept, openly, without shame. Medard bore up better. It was not his horse that had been spoiled. It was Lieutenant Foster's.", "Robert did what Robert always did, and looked around for a man to knock down. There was nobody in sight at all to take it out on.", "The bumpkins were long gone. They had tucked themselves away in some safe, quiet corner to enjoy the thing at leisure, and by that hour the deceitful fair ones were surely sitting beside them, laughing along in the mirth and the exultation.", "The unlucky gallants got up on what was left of their horses and pointed themselves toward home. No sorrier or more crestfallen cavalcade ever crossed that country. The animals themselves seemed to understand that they had been used exactly as King David's messengers were used by the evil-disposed Hanun; they carried their heads low, and would plainly have preferred to have \"tarried at Jericho\" a while longer. And there was no quiet road home in those days, no back lane by which a man might slip in unseen. The way ran out over open prairie with the whole community free to watch it. Worse: stirring events being scarce out there, the entire settlement kept the habit of turning out in a body to greet any new-comer who appeared.", "They retreated as fast as retreat could decently be managed, under a hail of shouting and jeering and mock condolence from everyone they knew; and the record shows that not one of those three young gentlemen was ever again in any hurry to accept an invitation to a festivity at Hickory Creek."],
  p2s40: ["The Napoleon showed herself at last. (A pity that so great a name has to be carried in the feminine gender.) There was no harbor at Chicago in those days. A ship stood off and dropped anchor beyond the bar — the tongue of land that made the left bank of the river — and everything she took or gave up had to be rowed to her, boats going out and coming back through the river mouth some way below.", "Which meant that loading fast was everything. A vessel wanted to be full and ready the moment a fair wind came, so that she could quit an anchorage that was exposed and hazardous.", "So we had been living out of our packed boxes for days on end. Our only plan was to see our friends safely aboard and then set out on our own road back to Fort Winnebago.", "The weighty things — furniture, trunks, all of it — had gone into the Napoleon already, to come round to us later by way of the Fox River. What we kept back was the little that would ride on a pack-horse, or in the light dearborn wagon Mr. Kercheval had lately fetched out from Detroit. It was the first luxury of that kind the prairies had ever seen. My husband had bought it so that his mother and his little nephew could travel in some comfort.", "Moving the goods of that many families of officers and soldiers, a boat-load at a stretch, together with the thousand odds and ends that a complete removal drags behind it, took an enormous amount of hauling and an enormous amount of time. But it was done. Every last article went out over the water and into the hold. Nothing was left standing on the shore except the passengers, and their turn came next.", "That was a considerable relief. Captain Hinckley had spent hours in a fever and a fuss, promising a change in the weather and grumbling that the number of boat-loads was more than any reasonable person required.", "Whoever happened to be looking lakeward just then — the schooner had lain about half a mile out — had the satisfaction of watching her put up sail and leave her station for open water. Anyone who looked a little later saw only a shape drawing off into the distance, as though she had taken aboard everything she ever meant to take. And there we stood. There we might go on standing for a week or more, at the pleasure of Captain Hinckley, and the schooner Napoleon, and a stiff east wind that showed no sign of tiring.", "Food was not the problem; there was plenty to be had. The problem was cooking it and eating it, and people met that difficulty however they could. Mrs. Engle, in her rooms upstairs, took her breakfast off a shingle with her husband's jack-knife, and when she had finished she sent the shingle and the knife down to Lieutenant Foster so that he might be accommodated too.", "We were lodged in the old mansion on the north side, and word ran up the river fast enough: the Napoleon had sailed off with \"the plunder\" and left the people standing on the beach. Before long Mrs. Portier — our kind Victoire — had furnished us with dishes and knives and forks and every other convenience our mess-basket could not supply.", "Two days of this. Then, early one fine morning, the word went through the place like wild-fire: the Napoleon was at anchor again, out beyond the bar.", "Nobody wasted a minute of it this time. By early afternoon we had said our goodbyes to friends we loved, and they were standing out from Chicago.[44]"],
};
